1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:43,280
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard.

7
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:49,280
Today's conversation is shorter than usual and will be featured in RevenueCat's State of Subscription Apps report.

8
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:57,040
Each episode in this series will explore one crucial metric and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

9
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,400
With me today, Greg Stewart, CEO of Ladder.

10
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:14,800
On the podcast, I talk with Greg about why optimizing for user success drives more revenue than conversion hacks, how to maximize the impact of annual plans, and why relying too heavily on discounts is a trap.

11
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:17,440
Hey Greg, it's nice to have you back on the podcast.

12
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,720
I'm looking forward to chatting today.

13
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:19,840
Thanks for having me.

14
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:20,880
Awesome to be here.

15
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:23,360
We're huge fans of the podcast and glad we could do it again.

16
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:28,400
So one of the big sections in the upcoming report is LTV.

17
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:40,800
And so we're going to share all sorts of stats around what we see patterns we see in different cohorts and different segments and different time frames of how our customers are monetizing their users.

18
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:47,440
How do you as one of the fastest growing fitness apps out there right now, how do you think about LTV?

19
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:55,760
Yeah, we don't spend a ton of time thinking about LTV as a metric for a couple of reasons and then we'll talk about what we do look at.

20
00:01:56,080 --> 00:02:02,960
But LTV for us is a little too wissy washy and too macro to make decisions on.

21
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:15,440
If you think about LTV and you're looking at some of your oldest cohorts, the product was different, many things changed by the time you've got a good read on any one cohort, the whole picture could have changed.

22
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:20,720
So it's not close enough to real time to help inform the decisions of the business.

23
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:29,840
We manage the day to day business on payback period, which is a far cleaner, very tangible metric that we can talk to the whole team about.

24
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,520
We invest in our growth, that growth gets paid back over time.

25
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:41,360
We understand the cash flow dynamics and what we're able to pay and invest in based on the expectation on when that investment is recouped.

26
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:44,800
So far more real time from a growth perspective.

27
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:49,920
Instead of LTV, we spend a ton of time thinking about retention, obviously.

28
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:58,080
It's an extremely consumer oriented retention minded team and business and app, and that's been true from the beginning.

29
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:03,120
Fitness is easy where there's a natural retention metric, which is workouts.

30
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:05,600
If you don't start a workout, you can't complete it..

31
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:10,000
If you don't complete workouts in Ladder, you're not going to join, you're not going to continue on as a member.

32
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:18,080
Everything that we build, every feature that we contemplate inside the app is all aimed at incremental workout completions.

33
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:24,160
It's all aimed at keeping you around the app, keeping you motivated to continue on and stay consistent with your plan.

34
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:36,400
That as a metric looking at workouts is far more tangible and easier to predict kind of the outcome of what's happening at the user level versus thinking about LTV.

35
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:39,200
High workouts equals high LTV.

36
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:42,160
So these things lead to LTV as an output metric.

37
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:46,400
But really hard to control input metrics, so you're just anchoring to lifetime value.

38
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:54,080
I love that you brought this up and maybe some people are listening, like, well, why did RevenueCat then do a whole section of their report on LTV?

39
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:00,000
But I think that's actually kind of key to properly contextualizing what LTV is.

40
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:04,240
So even though you don't think of it in terms of LTV, you are measuring payback period.

41
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:09,840
At RevenueCat, we have a chart and you probably look at this day X realized LTV is kind of what we call it.

42
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:16,720
So you can check in at different points, like thirty days, you know how much revenue you've realized from that cohort.

43
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:19,440
Yeah, like we are looking at that and that is payback.

44
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:20,160
period, right?

45
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:25,440
At the end of month one, at the end of month three, at the end of month six, at the end of the year, how much has been recovered?

46
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,160
Are you profitable on that user, that unit?

47
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:35,360
So we are certainly staring at that lifetime value in terms of what is that customer worth over the three years that they've been a member.

48
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:41,600
We have a estimate and a number there, but it's just not, it doesn't give us the ability to make any decisions around it.

49
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:45,760
So it's just not practical from a day to day management of the business.

50
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:55,440
We do spend a ridiculous amount of time kind of understanding retention at various kinds of points in our customer's journey.

51
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:57,920
And it all starts with who's coming into the app.

52
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:07,040
Like we spend a lot of time defining at a very granular level who are the relevant personas for Ladder and the programs that we have.

53
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:08,320
We call them teams.

54
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:09,680
Teams are led by a coach.

55
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:19,680
Each one of those teams represents a unique persona, a unique workout style that we are speaking to directly in creative that's aimed at that particular audience.

56
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:23,040
So we're very, very focused on speaking to the right user.

57
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:30,720
Users that we know are more likely to find success and long term success with Ladder even before they're in the app.

58
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:44,720
And we use the quiz, which we talked about in the last pod podcast, which collects a handful of questions to understand the fitness profile of a lead coming through, how many workouts they're doing, what modalities they like, what equipment they train with, where they want to train.

59
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:48,960
And we have a really good sense in real time, who's coming through our funnel.

60
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:53,200
And we cater to multiple personas and we can see that in real time.

61
00:05:53,680 --> 00:06:00,000
On one side of the spectrum, we have Pilates influenced at home with a dumbbell, with a lot of women who are newer to strength.

62
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:04,080
On the other side, we have advanced gym goers, full access to equipment and machines.

63
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:09,040
They know what they're doing when they show up and they're looking for a easier way to plan the workout itself.

64
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:16,080
So very, very different pers personas that we're speaking to uniquely in different creatives on social platforms.

65
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:25,920
So step one is getting to the right people and really making sure that you understand the pain points and how our solution addresses those pain points at the persona level.

66
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:29,120
And the quiz and our marketing is all geared towards that.

67
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,320
In the app itself, we have a seven day free trial.

68
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:37,520
We intentionally don't ask for a credit card in advance, which we didn't know was unique when we launched.

69
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,280
It turns out it's pretty unique.

70
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:43,840
We just had a hunch that as a consumer-oriented team, it's a better experience.

71
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:50,960
We launched the business in the middle of COVVID, there was an app being built every single day in our space.

72
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:53,680
It was very hard to stand out to the capital markets.

73
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:56,320
It was very hard to get the consumers' attention.

74
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:01,600
And we knew we had a superior product from very early in our life cycle as a business.

75
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:07,280
So we wanted to create an entry point into the app with little to no friction.

76
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:14,400
Like, let's use the product to move people through the funnel to do the selling, to work people through one or two workouts.

77
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:18,320
Then, ultimately, if that happens, the likelihood to pay is very high.

78
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:25,520
So we used the product to convince, not getting them to pay and then hoping that something happens after the fact.

79
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:33,360
So we spent a ton of time and continue to spend time iterating on that experience of what happens when you come into the app.

80
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:35,120
What's the first thing you see?

81
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:38,720
How do we get you through the first workout in the right program as quickly as possible?

82
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:40,320
How do we get you through a second workout?

83
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:44,240
How do we introduce these features that are not typical in fitness apps?

84
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:49,440
We have chats and social oriented experiences that aren't in other apps like us.

85
00:07:49,680 --> 00:08:02,080
So we have to figure out the right moments to introduce these experiences and to make sure that we don't overwhelm our member who's coming in with some context but not yet committed on Ladder as a solution for them.

86
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:20,960
So the trial is really, really important and we can we can pretty accurately predict what will happen from a conversion perspective, a week one workouts perspective, monthly retention perspective based on how the trial starts, the level of engagement, the number of workouts that they're completing and that continues into the membership.

87
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:25,680
Week one is an incredibly important kind of fork in the road for us.

88
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:32,800
We're solving for three workouts per week per member and most of the iteration is happening around that first week.

89
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:38,560
If you're hitting that metric in the first week, the odds of doing it again in the second week are exponentially higherher.

90
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:42,720
The odds of retaining at the end of one month if you're a monthly member are very high.

91
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:55,680
And so we use workout completions both in activation in first week and then beyond to understand the retention profile of our members and to understand the retention profile of cohorts of users that are coming in over time.

92
00:08:56,080 --> 00:09:01,120
In that first week, you've already collected a ton of data to understand kind of what persona you have.

93
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:05,840
And then you start seeing kind of the early signals of potentially being a good fit.

94
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:09,440
How deep does this segmentation and customization go?

95
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:11,360
Are you showing different paywalls?

96
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:12,640
Are you showing different offers?

97
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:18,960
How deep do you go with that segmentation to get people to that place where they are going to start paying.

98
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:20,160
Not on the happy path.

99
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:26,000
So if you come in off the quiz, you download the app, you start a trial, you start completing workouts.

100
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:31,520
We're not really trying to optimize or configure the paywall against what's happening.

101
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:36,800
Most of our happy path conversions are happening in the first two weeks post trial expiration.

102
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:43,520
Where we do segment on workouts and offers is if folks don't commit and don't convert right after the trial.

103
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:49,760
And we do have a significant number of people in any one cohort that will convert down the road because fitness is very personal.

104
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:56,560
And just because it wasn't relevant today, it doesn't mean mean you won't have a need come spring, summer, fall, the following January.

105
00:09:57,040 --> 00:10:00,720
So we have lots of shots at bat with our leads to convert them over time.

106
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:14,240
And we'll think about offers and coupons and the right mix of urgency levers based on the number of workouts that they completed in trial as a proxy for how much context they have coming in.

107
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:17,760
If you haven't completed a first workout, you really don't understand what Ladder is.

108
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:23,760
You don't understand the concept of a team that it's programming, that it's progressive overload trading, that it's not a library of workouts.

109
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:24,560
You're on a team.

110
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:26,320
You don't know if it's the right team or not.

111
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:31,840
So we have to both configure that experience when they do pay to someone who has very little context.

112
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:38,000
But even before that, we need to market to that user knowing that they're not sold yet on the core value prop itself.

113
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:39,200
How does that play out?

114
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:55,600
I know you've been, and I think we talked about this either in person one time when we were hanging out or maybe even on the last podcast that you've been shifting a lot of your subscribers from monthly, which is what you used to kind of promote more, to annual as a way to increase that LTV.

115
00:10:55,840 --> 00:11:01,040
So in the report, we're going to be sharing like 14 day LTV and 60 day realised LTV.

116
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:08,560
So how are you thinking about that kind of balance between monthly and annual and getting those payback periods reduced.

117
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:17,040
Yeah, our payback periods are very, very tight and we've over time increased the penetration of annual members in our subscriber base, which has been exciting.

118
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:21,920
The first rep of that was on price and that was a bunch of testing and iteration.

119
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:28,000
And then we got to a place where we thought we had the right clearing price for folks they were willing to commit upfront.

120
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:40,800
And then we did some experimentation around the paywall, anchoring to annual, putting incentives and added features into the annual membership to entice folks to commit on a longer time frame.

121
00:11:41,280 --> 00:12:28,480
And then when we are close to the end of a trial for any user and you actually have completed a workout, we'll first introduce the annual offer as a hey did you no, you know there's a cheaper way on a monthly basis to purchase ladder you get a significant discount you get added features and you're committing yourself to a year which is good for motivation and accountability so certainly something that we've spent time on and not it's now more annual members in our subscriber base than monthly and that wasn't true the last time we talked so in that segmentation are you presenting the monthly to certain cohorts and the annual to others or are you now more focused on that annual product and trying to drive as many users as possible to the annual or the fact that it is a kind of bigger, one time commitment, do you have to find that happy balance?

122
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:29,920
Both I would say.

123
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:33,600
For folks that completed zero workouts in trial, we don't push annual.

124
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,280
The context isn't there.

125
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:37,520
We're expensive versus most apps in our space.

126
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:44,320
You don't know enough on why the price is the price and why it's worth the value versus what you're solving for.

127
00:12:44,640 --> 00:12:47,120
So for zero workouts, we point to monthly.

128
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:58,640
For those that have got through key activation moments, first workout, second workout, we'll introduce and socialize and lean into annual just before trial expires and right after trial expires.

129
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:06,400
If that offer isn't taken or the conversion doesn't happen, then we start moving to monthly offers.

130
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:08,640
But the monthly offers are all inter offers.

131
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:13,360
They're just a discount on the first month that then clicks up to full price on month two.

132
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:19,760
So it's to lower the hurdle to get into the product and to really experience it in the first couple weeks of membership.

133
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:22,000
And then it clicks up to full price after that.

134
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:27,360
Are you making those same kind of offers to those folks who didn't complete a workout?

135
00:13:27,520 --> 00:13:28,400
I think I saw a promo.

136
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:31,680
It's like five dollars or ten dollars or something for that first month.

137
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:39,200
Do those people that you offer that five dollar offer to, are they less retentive than the people who were willing to kind of jump in and pay off the bat?

138
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:59,200
One important point I would make is if you stacked up all the time on iteration between activation in the product and using our product and levers that we know are correlated to workout completions versus iteration on deals, it would probably be 90% product, 10% deals.

139
00:13:59,440 --> 00:14:11,440
So we spent a lot of time moving the needle on the percentage of people that get through one workout, the percentage of people that get through two workouts, and the conversion rate is extremely high for folks that get through those moments.

140
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:16,400
So we have a lot of atypical levers in our app versus fitness app.

141
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:23,360
And I can give you an anecdotal story is we have a feature where you can cheer on other folks who are working out.

142
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:32,160
You open the app on the home screen, you'll see avatars on the top, you'll see a yellow volt circle, and it represents how far as the workout that person is.

143
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:36,640
And you can send them cheers by double tapping and pick an emoji that you want to send.

144
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:42,960
Seems very simple, but we correlated the number of cheers received to workout completions and long-term retention.

145
00:14:43,440 --> 00:15:03,200
And so we realized the importance of this feature and people feeling like there are others that are doing it with them and feeling supportive that we now use that to figure out how we incentivize and increase the number of outbound cheers that on the receiving end, you're more likely to receive a basket of cheers in your experience, especially early in the trial.

146
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:17,680
So we spent a lot of time thinking about what are the levers that we have to drive incremental workout completions that ultimately get people to the value prop of ladder of having a plan and ultimately help convert them or increase the likelihood that they'll convert.

147
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:20,720
So most of our brain space has gone towards that.

148
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:24,400
We do have built in kind of moments in our calendar.

149
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:30,400
We'll spend a little bit more time thinking about the iteration side of that.

150
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:33,120
We program workouts on six week cycles.

151
00:15:33,200 --> 00:15:34,560
We call them string series.

152
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:48,160
And they become like these great entry point moments into the product where you can tell a story about urgency, saying, you weren't here or it didn't make sense for you to join for the first string series, but tomorrow is day one and it's a great time to hop in.

153
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:58,560
And we have a whole motion from a marketing perspective through the platform mobile, through our coaches in the app, all telling a story about the next series, what we're working on, what themes are at the platform level.

154
00:15:58,960 --> 00:16:09,680
That gives us the ability to then go into our database and tell a story on urgency and use discount and coupon to lower the barrier to entry to incentivize people to get in and experience the value proposed.

155
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:13,920
So we have moments where it's more impactful to experiment on the deal side.

156
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:24,000
The five dollar deal is like a good, good example where people who claim or take advantage of the five dollar offer, it's only about half of those that are retained in month two.

157
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:25,120
And it's pretty consistent.

158
00:16:25,280 --> 00:16:26,880
Now this is the minority of conversions.

159
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:30,960
The small basket of conversions is people that didn't get through the product in the trial.

160
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:33,680
But of that group, about half are retained.

161
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:36,320
But that half clicks up to full price.

162
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:38,880
So they go from five to twenty nine dollars ninety nine cents.

163
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:50,080
So in the first month, it's a really nice spike in user growth and in the second month, it's growth in ARR for that cohort, even though it's a smaller basket of users that make it through the first month.

164
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:53,120
All right, Greg, thank you so much for all those insights.

165
00:16:53,280 --> 00:16:55,280
Is there anything else you wanted to share as we're wrapping up?

166
00:16:55,600 --> 00:17:03,040
I would say if you want to see some of the learnings in real life that we just talked about, especially in trial and activation, I would try the app.

167
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:07,520
There's no credit card trial as we talked about, so very easy to get in there.

168
00:17:08,080 --> 00:17:11,920
I think if you get through that first workout, the odds of us seeing you as a number are very high.

169
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:16,640
So we'd love to have everyone try the product and let us know what you think.

170
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:17,120
Awesome.

171
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:20,800
We're going to see a lot of super fit subscription app folks next year.

172
00:17:22,160 --> 00:17:23,680
Well, Greg, thanks so much for joining me.

173
00:17:24,080 --> 00:17:24,960
It was a great conversation.

174
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:25,840
I appreciate it.

175
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:26,640
Thanks for having me.

176
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:30,320
Thanks so much for listening.

177
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:34,720
If you have a minute, please leave a review in your favorite podcast player.

178
00:17:35,600 --> 00:17:40,400
You can also stop by chat dot subclub dot com to join our private community.
